  "live_records": {
    "q-252923": {
      "dom_id": "step-q252923",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $B=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${e}^{B}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and the given matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{B}={e}^{PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({e}^{J})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{J}=[\\begin{aligned}f(3) & {f}^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & {e}^{3} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{B} & =P\\cdot ({e}^{J})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & {e}^{3} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -2{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3} & -4{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3} & -{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
