    "q-253391": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253391",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{3}(2))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
